The dispatcher, v1 to v2

This section is written for the reader who wants to reproduce the dispatch record behind the paper rather than take it on trust. The record was produced by a shell script that sends a brief to a worker, applies an acceptance test to what comes back, and writes one row into an instrument table for each dispatch. Between v1 and v2 of that script the meaning of a row changed, and the change is load-bearing for anyone reading the table: a row that says OK under v1 and a row that says OK under v2 are not the same claim. This section sets out what v1 did, what was wrong with it, what v2 does instead, and how the two row populations should be read. It is deliberately conservative about facts: where a detail a reproducer would want is not recorded here, the gap is named rather than filled.

Begin with v1 as it ran. Its file is hf_dispatch_v1_as_run.sh, and its central defect is visible in the order of two operations: the script wrote the instrument row before its acceptance test ran. The verdict column recorded the worker process's exit status and nothing else, on a two-value scale, OK if the process exited zero and TIMEOUT otherwise. The needle outcome was not logged at all. Two consequences follow for anyone reading v1-era rows. First, whatever the acceptance test found could not have altered the record, because the row had already been written by the time the test ran. Second, the vocabulary had no slot for anything but a clean run or a hang: a process that exited non-zero for any reason was filed as TIMEOUT, and a process that exited zero was filed as OK regardless of whether the needle ever appeared in the response.

The exit-status verdict would matter less if the acceptance test itself were sound. It was not, and the reason sits in how v1 invoked the worker CLI. The invocation was hermes chat -q followed by the brief in quotes. On that path the CLI echoes the full brief to standard output before the response, draws a box around the response, and appends an exit summary. The stream v1 captured was therefore not the response but a full transcript of the exchange. Every brief ended with a trailer naming its needle, so the transcript began with text containing the needle before the worker had produced a word. The v1 acceptance test searched the whole transcript for the needle, and the echo alone could satisfy it. The incident is recorded as INC-008. In the worst case a dispatch could produce a row reading OK, written before the test ran, on the strength of a zero exit status and a needle string the dispatcher itself had placed on the wire.

A second, quieter discovery concerns line endings. The CLI's terminal library writes CRLF line endings when standard output is a file rather than a terminal, which is exactly the situation a capture harness creates. A hand-written test fixture missed this, so the fixture did not reproduce what the harness captures in the field. The fixtures are now generated by the same print call that produces the real captures, and the generated set includes a CRLF case, so the matching logic is exercised against the line endings it will actually meet. The general point is worth stating for reproducers: any matching test built on hand-typed assumptions about a captured stream should be replaced by fixtures produced by the same code path that produces the stream.

v2 rebuilds the pipeline in three places: what is captured, what counts as a hit, and when the row is written. The capture change is the invocation. v2 calls hermes chat with the -Q flag together with -q, and the brief still travels through -q. On this path the CLI prints the final response alone to standard output and writes the session id to standard error. What the harness captures is the response and nothing else: no echo of the brief, no box, no exit summary. The flaw behind INC-008 is removed at the source. There is no transcript for an echo to satisfy, because there is no echo.

The matching change is positional. v2 normalises carriage returns out of the capture and then requires the needle to be the last non-empty response line. A needle in the middle of the response does not pass, and a needle anywhere except at the end does not pass. This gate is stricter than v1's whole-transcript search twice over, once because it demands a position and once because it operates on response-only output.

The recording change is ordering and vocabulary. v2 writes the row after the acceptance gate has run, so a row exists only where a verdict exists. The verdict column takes one of four values, OK, NEEDLE-MISS, TIMEOUT, or ERROR, and every row also carries the numeric exit code, the brief size in bytes, the number of bytes printed back to the hub, and a per-dispatch key under which the raw .out and .err files are retained. A reproducer who doubts a verdict can move from the row to the retained files and check it by hand. That audit path is the largest single gain over v1, where the needle outcome was absent from the record.

One mechanical detail belongs beside recording: locking. v2 guards concurrent runs with a lock created by mkdir rather than flock, because flock is absent on macOS. If the lock times out, the script writes a pending file instead of proceeding. A reproducer on another platform should know the lock is directory-based before substituting a different mechanism and changing the failure behaviour.

Two failure classes are deliberately treated differently. If the worker process exits non-zero after exec, meaning after the worker has actually been launched, v2 writes an ERROR row and keeps the files, so the failure enters the instrument and can be audited. If a script precondition fails, the script writes no row and exits 2. The precondition list is: a missing or unreadable brief, a kanban variable set, hermes not on PATH, or an unknown worker. The distinction is chronological. Preconditions are checked before any dispatch attempt exists, so there is nothing to instrument, while a post-exec failure is a real attempt and belongs in the record as an ERROR row. For counting, this means the instrument holds one row per dispatch that passed the preconditions and reached the gate, and an exit code of 2 from the script is the signature of a precondition failure that produced no row. The kanban variable itself is not named in this section.

Where does the record stand? Exactly one live v2 dispatch has run. It completed in 9 seconds, the response was 449 bytes, and the needle hit, so its row reads OK. One run proves the mechanics, not a track record, and every v1 row in the instrument predates all of the safeguards described above and should be read under v1 rules. Several facts a reproducer will eventually want are not recorded here: the filename of the v2 script, the date of the single live v2 dispatch, the worker it targeted, and the number of v1 rows in the instrument. None of those should be assumed. What this section does establish is a reading rule. Trust a v2 OK only after opening the retained .out and .err files under the row's per-dispatch key and confirming that, after carriage-return normalisation, the needle is the last non-empty line of the response. That check takes a minute, and it is the difference between reproducing the record and retyping it.

AB-S1-OK
